# Solution

## Part a) Centre of gravity of the astroid arc in the first quadrant

**Parametrisation.** The astroid $x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}=a^{2/3}$ is parametrised by
$$x=a\cos^{3}t,\qquad y=a\sin^{3}t,\qquad t\in[0,\pi/2].$$

**Arc-length element.**
$$\dot x=-3a\cos^{2}t\sin t,\quad \dot y=3a\sin^{2}t\cos t,$$
$$\dot s^{2}=9a^{2}\cos^{2}t\sin^{2}t(\cos^{2}t+\sin^{2}t)=9a^{2}\cos^{2}t\sin^{2}t,$$
so $\dfrac{ds}{dt}=3a\cos t\sin t$.

**Total length.**
$$L=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}3a\cos t\sin t\,dt=3a\Big[\frac{\sin^{2}t}{2}\Big]_{0}^{\pi/2}=\frac{3a}{2}.$$

**Symmetry.** The first-quadrant arc is symmetric about the line $y=x$ (swapping $x\leftrightarrow y$ leaves the curve invariant), hence $\bar x=\bar y$. It suffices to compute $\bar x$.

**First moment.**
$$\int x\,ds=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}a\cos^{3}t\cdot 3a\cos t\sin t\,dt=3a^{2}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\cos^{4}t\sin t\,dt=3a^{2}\Big[-\frac{\cos^{5}t}{5}\Big]_{0}^{\pi/2}=\frac{3a^{2}}{5}.$$

**Centre of gravity.**
$$\bar x=\frac{1}{L}\int x\,ds=\frac{1}{3a/2}\cdot\frac{3a^{2}}{5}=\frac{2a}{5}.$$

By symmetry $\bar y=\dfrac{2a}{5}$.

$$\boxed{\left(\bar x,\bar y\right)=\left(\frac{2a}{5},\,\frac{2a}{5}\right).}$$

---

## Part b) Centre of gravity of the area bounded by $y^{2}=2px$ and $x^{2}=2py$

**Intersection points.** From $y^{2}=2px$ we have $x=\dfrac{y^{2}}{2p}$. Substituting into $x^{2}=2py$:
$$\frac{y^{4}}{4p^{2}}=2py\;\Longrightarrow\;y^{4}=8p^{3}y\;\Longrightarrow\;y\big(y^{3}-8p^{3}\big)=0,$$
giving $y=0$ (point $(0,0)$) and $y=2p$ (point $(2p,2p)$).

**Description of the region.** For $y\in[0,2p]$, the right boundary is $x_{R}=\sqrt{2py}$ (from $x^{2}=2py$) and the left boundary is $x_{L}=\dfrac{y^{2}}{2p}$ (from $y^{2}=2px$). Indeed at $y=p$: $x_{R}=\sqrt{2}\,p>x_{L}=p/2$.

**Symmetry.** The two curves are exchanged by the swap $x\leftrightarrow y$, so the bounded region is symmetric about $y=x$; hence $\bar x=\bar y$.

**Area.**
$$A=\int_{0}^{2p}\!\Big(\sqrt{2py}-\frac{y^{2}}{2p}\Big)dy=\sqrt{2p}\cdot\frac{2}{3}(2p)^{3/2}-\frac{1}{2p}\cdot\frac{(2p)^{3}}{3}=\frac{8p^{2}}{3}-\frac{4p^{2}}{3}=\frac{4p^{2}}{3}.$$

**Moment about the $y$-axis.**
$$M_{y}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{2p}\!\big(x_{R}^{2}-x_{L}^{2}\big)\,dy=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{2p}\!\Big(2py-\frac{y^{4}}{4p^{2}}\Big)dy=\frac{1}{2}\Big(4p^{3}-\frac{8p^{3}}{5}\Big)=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{12p^{3}}{5}=\frac{6p^{3}}{5}.$$

**Moment about the $x$-axis.**
$$M_{x}=\int_{0}^{2p}y\Big(\sqrt{2py}-\frac{y^{2}}{2p}\Big)dy=\sqrt{2p}\cdot\frac{2}{5}(2p)^{5/2}-\frac{1}{2p}\cdot\frac{(2p)^{4}}{4}=\frac{16p^{3}}{5}-2p^{3}=\frac{6p^{3}}{5}.$$

(As expected, $M_{x}=M_{y}$ by the $y=x$ symmetry.)

**Centre of gravity.**
$$\bar x=\frac{M_{y}}{A}=\frac{6p^{3}/5}{4p^{2}/3}=\frac{9p}{10},\qquad \bar y=\frac{M_{x}}{A}=\frac{9p}{10}.$$

$$\boxed{\left(\bar x,\bar y\right)=\left(\frac{9p}{10},\,\frac{9p}{10}\right).}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
